        {
          "duration": {
            "from": 338.116125,
            "to": 348.116125
          },
          "pause": true,
          "displayType": "poster",
          "buttonOnMobile": false,
          "label": "<p>Paediatric rheumatic diseases are broadly classified into autoimmune and autoinf</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>Paediatric rheumatic diseases are broadly classified into autoimmune and autoinflammatory groups. Which of the following is driven primarily by the innate immune system?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Systemic lupus erythematosus</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>SLE is autoimmune — the adaptive immune system produces auto-antibodies against nuclear material, and immune complexes deposit in organs.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Systemic JIA</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Systemic JIA is autoinflammatory, with the innate immune system driving excess production of IL-1β, IL-6, and TNF-α rather than generating auto-antibodies.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Juvenile dermatomyositis</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Juvenile dermatomyositis is autoimmune, involving the adaptive immune system with auto-antibodies directed against muscle and skin components.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Polyarticular RF-positive JIA</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>RF-positive polyarticular JIA is autoimmune — it is the paediatric counterpart of adult rheumatoid arthritis, driven by adaptive immunity and auto-antibody production.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "Systemic JIA is autoinflammatory (innate immune overactivation producing IL-1β, IL-6, TNF-α). SLE, juvenile dermatomyositis, and RF-positive polyarticular JIA are autoimmune, driven by adaptive immunity and auto-antibodies."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Correct! Systemic JIA, Kawasaki disease, IgA vasculitis, and familial Mediterranean fever are all autoinflammatory — the innate immune system drives inflammation without classical auto-antibody production."
                }
              ],
              "behaviour": {
                "enableRetry": true,
