          },
          "pause": true,
          "displayType": "poster",
          "buttonOnMobile": false,
          "label": "<p>A 6-year-old boy presents with a raised, non-blanching rash on his buttocks and </p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>A 6-year-old boy presents with a raised, non-blanching rash on his buttocks and legs, ankle swelling, and colicky abdominal pain. His platelet count is normal. Which investigation screens for the complication that determines long-term outcome in this condition?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Coagulation profile</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Coagulation studies are typically normal in HSP. The purpura results from IgA-mediated vasculitis (inflamed vessel walls), not a clotting factor deficiency.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Abdominal ultrasound</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>An ultrasound is important to exclude intussusception when abdominal pain is severe, but this is an acute complication — not the one that drives long-term prognosis.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Urinalysis for blood and protein</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Renal involvement — detected as haematuria or proteinuria on urinalysis — is the most important determinant of long-term outcome in HSP. It usually appears within the first two months.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Bone marrow aspiration</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>The normal platelet count rules out a primary marrow disorder. HSP purpura is vasculitic in origin, not a consequence of marrow failure.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "While an abdominal ultrasound may be needed acutely, the complication that most influences long-term outcome in HSP is renal involvement — detected by urinalysis showing haematuria or proteinuria."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Correct! Kidney involvement is the complication that matters most for long-term prognosis in HSP. Urinalysis for blood and protein should be monitored regularly, especially in the first two months."
                }
              ],
              "behaviour": {
