        {
          "duration": {
            "from": 895.8910833333333,
            "to": 905.8910833333333
          },
          "pause": true,
          "displayType": "poster",
          "buttonOnMobile": false,
          "label": "<p>A 4-year-old girl with oligoarticular JIA has a positive ANA titre of 1:160. Her</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>A 4-year-old girl with oligoarticular JIA has a positive ANA titre of 1:160. Her eyes appear completely normal on external examination. What is the most appropriate next step?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Reassure the family — no eye follow-up is needed since the eyes look normal</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Chronic anterior uveitis in oligoarticular JIA is often a 'white eye' — completely normal externally while inflammation silently damages the structures inside. Missing it leads to irreversible vision loss.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Referral for slit-lamp examination by an ophthalmologist</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>ANA positivity in oligoarticular JIA is the strongest predictor of chronic anterior uveitis. These children need slit-lamp screening every three months, even when the eyes appear entirely normal.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Start systemic corticosteroids immediately</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Systemic corticosteroids are not indicated at this stage. The first step is to confirm whether uveitis is present through a slit-lamp examination before deciding on treatment.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Order anti-dsDNA antibodies to investigate lupus</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>A positive ANA in a child with established oligoarticular JIA signals uveitis risk, not necessarily lupus. Anti-dsDNA testing is reserved for when lupus is specifically suspected on clinical grounds.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "A positive ANA in oligoarticular JIA is a red flag for silent chronic anterior uveitis. The child needs referral for a slit-lamp exam by an ophthalmologist every three months, regardless of whether the eyes look normal externally."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Correct! ANA-positive children with oligoarticular JIA must have regular slit-lamp examinations — the eye can look entirely normal while chronic anterior uveitis silently damages it."
                }
              ],
